[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Bigger batteries take longer to charge, right? We charge our phones in under an hour and leave the EV plugged in overnight. But what if today\'s batteries are the exception, not the rule? Researchers in Australia [music] just built and tested a battery that turns everything we thought we knew about them'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'upside down. The larger they made it, the faster [music] it charged. You\'ve heard of quantum computers. Well, this is a quantum battery. By operating at a quantum scale, this battery juices up super fast in a super absorption event.'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And it gets weirder. The scientists who made this quantum battery, which is the very [music] first, didn\'t even charge it with electricity. They charged it with light from a laser. It\'s that kind of breakthrough that could revolutionize'

[00:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'how batteries get built [music] and what they can even do. So, how do quantum batteries work? And are they just a laboratory curiosity? Or might we someday [music] see them in our phones and cars? I\'m Matt Ferrell. Welcome to Undecided.'

[00:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This video [music] is brought to you by Micro Center. I talk a lot about the future batteries, like a lot a lot. But what I can say, &gt;&gt; [music]'

[01:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; battery talk just charges me up. But usually when I talk about the future batteries, I\'m talking about changes to battery chemistry, like the potential shift to solid-state batteries, which is a shift we\'re still waiting on. Now, quantum batteries go further than chemistry. We\'re talking about a'

[01:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'revolution in battery physics. A battery that charges more quickly the larger it is is kind of the weird I can\'t leave alone. I mean, like what\'s next? A car that goes more forward the faster it goes backwards?'

[01:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Kind of breaks my brain. Dr. James Quach from Australia\'s National Science Agency, CSIRO, built this proof-of-concept quantum battery with the help from the Royal Melbourne Institute of Technology or RMIT. It does a full battery cycle. It charges, stores'

[01:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'energy, and it discharges. But that\'s where the normal battery behavior ends. Instead of juicing it up with electricity, researchers use what physicists call quantum effects to charge it in a flash using light from a'

[01:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'laser. That sounds like the sci-fi dream of remote charging. Energy beamed in from a distance with no need for a cord or even a charging pad. Kwak told The Guardian, "Once the technology matures, you would no longer need to stop your'

[02:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'car at a petrol station to charge it up. You could charge it on the go." I don\'t know if that or some version of it will ever happen, but I do want to find out why a physicist thinks it could. And to do that, we need to answer a simpler'

[02:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'question. What exactly is a quantum battery and how does it charge all at once? First off, we\'ve got to clear up just what\'s meant by the quantum realm. The real one, not the one that the Marvel execs keep sending Paul Rudd to.'

[02:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Down at the scale of the atomic and subatomic particles, things get very weird and the classic laws of physics aren\'t enough to explain what\'s happening. Those laws can tell you exactly how a ball will fall, but they can\'t predict what a single electron'

[02:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'will do. For particles that small, we\'ve got a whole other handbook called quantum mechanics. A quantum is the smallest whole unit that can interact, [music] like a single photon, which is a single quantum of light. In atoms,'

[02:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'electrons gain or lose energy in whole units called quanta, too. Hit an electron with a photon and the electron\'s energy climbs up a quantum or two, like taking steps up a ladder. The electron always lands with its foot on a'

[03:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'specific [music] rung, never in between. And there\'s something funny about the ways that quanta of light and matter behave. Instead of acting individually, they begin to behave collectively as a system. And these collective effects are'

[03:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'why quantum batteries can charge faster when you add more storage units. That happens because the energy states in the storage units of quantum batteries are entangled. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; I think of entanglement like the synced up movements of dancers in a flash mob.'

[03:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'If you know what one dancer is doing, you probably know the dance move of the next guy over. In a quantum battery, the energy levels of electrons can get synced up or entangled, too. If one storage unit is jumping to a higher'

[03:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'energy state, they probably all are in a super fast charging event. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; And this is more than theory. Kwak demonstrated these collective effects in his first quantum half battery in 2022. Which half? Charging part, the fun with lasers.'

[03:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Before we talk about how we can charge a battery with just light, let\'s charge up today\'s sponsor, Micro Center. A quick confession, I\'m an Apple guy, but I\'ve been building PCs for decades and I still can\'t quit. My latest is a tiny'

[04:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'gaming rig for the living room hooked up to the big screen for my Steam library. My Mac Studio is the brain of my smart home. It runs Home Assistant, handles my automations, and powers my own self-hosted AI voice assistant. All local, all mine, none of it leaves the'

[04:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'house. For years, whenever I needed a part fast and [music] cheap, Micro Center was my move. I practically lived at the Cambridge store. So, I\'m happy they\'re sponsoring this one. Their back to school deals event is on now and the deals are genuinely good.'

[04:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The link\'s in the description if you want to browse. Two store updates, too. Austin, Texas, you\'re finally getting a Micro Center. Sign up with the link below for a free 128 gig flash drive and grab it when the doors open later this'

[04:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'year. And Columbus, Ohio, your store is getting a remodel. [music] Sign up for your own free 128 gig flash drive for the grand reopening later this year. And don\'t worry, it stays open the whole time. And for everything else tech, check out Micro Center news. All'

[04:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the links in the description and thanks to Micro Center and to all of you for keeping this channel going. Okay, let\'s get back to charging batteries with light. Working with a team in Italy and the UK, Quack used ultra-fast laser pulses to bump the'

[05:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'battery storage units from a low-energy ground state to a higher-energy excited state. The storage units were molecules of a dye called Lumogen F Orange, which jumps from a low to high-energy state as it absorbs [music] photons. And it'

[05:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sounds more delicious than it probably tastes. Although, actually, it looks like it tastes pretty good, too. In theory, the more dye molecules that you put into the battery, the faster it charges thanks to the collective effects. That\'s the exact'

[05:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'theory Quack\'s team set out to test. To build the battery, the team embedded dye molecules in a resin inside an optical cavity with special mirrors on the top and bottom that trap the photons inside. By testing different concentrations of'

[05:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the dye in the cavity, Quak was able to show that charging sped up when more dye was packed in. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Those orange and red lines, well, they\'re super absorption, super fast charging, the exact payoff scientists hoped for.'

[06:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; But the battery, it\'s it\'s tiny. How much energy did it even hold? We\'ll get to that. But even if it\'s small and very strange, this [music] quantum battery does what a battery should. It charges, stores energy, and discharges electricity. To make it, they'

[06:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'needed more than just an optical cavity. They needed a full battery sandwich with conductive layers that can convert stored energy into electrical current. I\'m so hungry for lunch. I could swear that\'s lettuce, cheese, and ham in that'

[06:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sandwich. But the middle bits are a dye called phthalo blue. Quak\'s team switched to phthalo blue for this full quantum battery because it\'s excited state is even longer lived. &gt;&gt; [music]'

[06:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; It lasts about six orders of magnitude longer than the pulse of light used to excite it. That\'s a million times longer, which sounds fantastic until you find out just how long that charging pulse lasted. Now, here\'s the part the quantum scientists never'

[06:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'predicted. Remember how the quantum battery charged faster as it got bigger? Well, it discharged faster, too. And the ability to push energy in or out super [music] fast means these batteries could store a lot of energy and deliver a lot'

[07:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of power. Might, someday. But today, a phone battery holds around 10 kJ of energy. This quantum battery prototype holds less than a nanojoule.'

[07:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It turns out there\'s an upper limit to how much dye they could pack into that optical cavity. Too much, and the dye molecules stopped supercharging with their neighbors and started [music] blocking each other in little like quantum backyard disputes. But Quak\'s team already has ideas for'

[07:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'other dyes that might play better with others. And although the battery held its charge six orders of magnitude longer than it charged, We\'re talking about incredibly short time scales. The battery took femtoseconds to charge.'

[07:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'That\'s [music] quadrillions of a second. And the energy was stored for just nanoseconds. That\'s not long enough for a proper telephone call. &gt;&gt; [laughter] &gt;&gt; Unless of course you\'re talking about a telemarketer on the line. So, we\'ve got on the one hand the'

[07:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'world\'s first full cycle quantum battery. On the other, it holds hardly any energy for any length of time. Feeling Schrader\'s experiment, I\'m simultaneously in total awe and total meh.'

[08:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But for a wild new battery design that was only proposed in 2012, the progress is impressive. One decade to prove the charge mechanism works and then just a few more years to build a whole actual battery. So, how close are we to putting'

[08:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'quantum batteries in an actual device? Kwok doesn\'t think quantum batteries are set to replace traditional batteries in everyday electronics anytime soon. He says the next steps are scaling up the battery size and lengthening the amount'

[08:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of time it holds on a charge. Obviously. Now, one way researchers are looking to increase storage time is to stack materials. One that super absorbs light and another that can hang on to that energy for much longer. Kwok\'s'

[08:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'teammate James Hutchinson at the University of Melbourne told Live Science that he expects quantum batteries to one day have a higher energy density and greater durability than today\'s batteries on top of super fast charging. Whether they\'ll ever hold'

[09:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'their charge long enough to be the battery in an EV is an open question. Again, obviously. Laser remote charging is surprisingly plowing ahead. Just last year we saw the US military\'s Advanced Research Agency,'

[09:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'DARPA, using infrared laser to beam energy to a spot more than 5 miles away. They transmitted 800 watts of electricity in under a minute to a receiver made of a solar panel at a distance of 8.6 km.'

[09:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, as skeptical as I am that we\'ll be charging our drones or EVs on the go with lasers, we\'re kind of already on the way. Sometimes science advances slowly and sometimes it beams down out of thin air. But long before quantum batteries show'

[09:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'up in cell phones or EVs, again, if they ever do, we might see them in quantum computers and quantum sensors first. That\'s because quantum batteries could be built right into quantum computers and even entangled with the qubits that'

[09:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'do the calculations. Yeah, like that. So, is this the future batteries? Maybe it is. Maybe it isn\'t. At the same time. But what do you think? You can also check out extended cuts of many of my'

